{%- doc -%}
  One panel of the product tabs section.

  The label lives here rather than on the section so a tab is one thing in the editor: rename it,
  reorder it, or fill it, all in the same place. The section reads these labels to draw the strip.

  Public on purpose: a private type (an underscore prefix) is not offered in "Add block", so the tabs
  could not be added from the editor at all.

  Content is whatever theme or app blocks the tab holds, which is what keeps this usable before and
  after the move to metaobjects: today a `custom-liquid` or `product-metafield-text` block reads
  `closest.product`, tomorrow the same slot takes a block that reads a metaobject entry, and the
  tab chrome does not change either way.
{%- enddoc -%}

{%- capture children -%}
  {% content_for 'blocks' %}
{%- endcapture -%}

<div
  class="product-tabs__panel"
  id="ProductTabPanel-{{ block.id }}"
  role="tabpanel"
  aria-labelledby="ProductTab-{{ block.id }}"
  tabindex="0"
  ref="panels[]"
  {%- comment -%}
    The label travels on the panel, read here where a block always sees its own settings. The
    section builds the strip from `section.blocks`, and a section that renders theme blocks through
    `content_for 'blocks'` does not read a child block's settings reliably from there - so the strip
    text is filled from this attribute by the component instead.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  data-tab-label="{{ block.settings.label | escape }}"
  {%- comment -%}
    A tab whose blocks render nothing is marked here so the strip can drop it.

    This is content-driven on purpose. A Brew Guide tab enabled once for the
    whole catalogue is empty on every coffee that has no brew data, and the
    alternative -- a per-product flag someone has to set and keep in step with
    the content -- goes stale the first time a metafield is filled in and
    nobody remembers the switch. Whatever the blocks inside actually render is
    the honest signal, and it works for Profile, Origin and Sourcing too, not
    just Brew Guide.

    The section cannot make this call: it builds the strip from
    `section.blocks` and, as the note above says, does not read a child theme
    block's settings reliably from there. The panel knows; the strip is told.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  {%- if children | strip == blank -%}data-tab-empty="true"{%- endif -%}
  {{ block.shopify_attributes }}
>
  {{ children }}
</div>

{% schema %}
{
  "name": "Tab",
  "tag": null,
  "blocks": [
    {
      "type": "@theme"
    },
    {
      "type": "@app"
    },
    {
      "type": "_divider"
    }
  ],
  "settings": [
    {
      "type": "text",
      "id": "label",
      "label": "t:settings.label",
      "info": "The wording in the tab strip. Left empty, the tab is numbered."
    }
  ],
  "presets": [
    {
      "name": "Tab"
    }
  ]
}
{% endschema %}
